Choosing the right collection.

You've met all the pieces; this section is the decision procedure that ties them together. In practice, picking a collection is two quick questions — what shape of access do I need, then what ordering, if any — plus a small set of defaults that are right most of the time.

Step one: what shape of access? Start from how you'll use the data, not how you'll store it. If you need a sequence you index into, or that keeps order or duplicates, that's a list. If you need membership or uniqueness — "is this in the set?" — that's a set. If you look a value up by a key, that's a map. And if you process in order — first-in-first-out, or as a stack, or by priority — that's a deque, or a priority-queue. That single question eliminates most of the framework immediately.

Step two: what ordering do you need? Within set and map, the implementation is chosen entirely by the ordering you want. If you don't care about order, use hash-set or hash-map — the fast, constant-time default. If you want to remember insertion order, use linked-hash-set or linked-hash-map. And if you need it kept sorted, or need range queries, use tree-set or tree-map, at order log-n. For a list, the choice is nearly always array-list; and for a queue or a stack, nearly always array-deque.

So here are the defaults, in one breath. List, array-list. Set, hash-set. Map, hash-map. Queue or stack, array-deque. Priority order, priority-queue. And enum keys or elements, enum-map or enum-set. Most production code is these six. The linked and tree variants are deliberate upgrades, for when order or sorting matters.

It helps to know the rough costs, so you don't pick a structure that's quietly order-n. Array-list: get and set are constant time; add-at-end is amortised constant; but insert or remove in the middle is order-n. Hash-map and hash-set: get, put, add, and contains are constant time on average, given a good hash code. Tree-map and tree-set: those same operations are order log-n, but you get sorting and range queries in return. Array-deque: constant time at both ends. And priority-queue: offer and poll are order log-n, while peek is constant.

And don't forget the cross-cutting habits, because the choice of type is only half of using collections well. Program to the interface — list, not array-list — in your fields, parameters, and returns. Prefer immutable collections — list-of and map-of for fixed data, copy-of for snapshots — and copy at your boundaries. Make sure elements and keys have correct equals and hash code, by making value types records, and keep your keys immutable. And use remove-if to delete while iterating, merge and compute-if-absent for map updates, and a comparator for ad-hoc orderings.

So here's the mental model to walk away with. The access shape picks the interface. The ordering picks the implementation. And the defaults — array-list, hash-map, hash-set, array-deque — are right until a concrete requirement says otherwise. That closes collections. Module five, generics, explains the angle brackets that have been quietly making all of this type-safe.
